.TH DIG 1
.SH NAME
dig \- inspect DNS records
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dig
.RI [ @SERVER ] " [NAME [TYPE]] [+short]"
.br
.B dig
.RI [ @SERVER ] " -x ADDRESS"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Query a numeric IPv4 DNS server, or the servers in /etc/resolv.conf.
The default query is A; with no NAME it is the root NS set. Types include
A, AAAA, MX, NS, CNAME, TXT, SOA, PTR, ANY and TYPE followed by a number.
-t TYPE is another spelling. +short prints answer data only. -x constructs
the IPv4 reverse-lookup name. The decoded result includes status, TTL and
answer, authority and additional sections.
.PP
Replies are checked against the server, query ID and exact question, and
compressed names and record lengths are bounded. Truncated UDP answers are
retried over TCP, up to a 4096-octet reply. No EDNS, DNSSEC validation, search
domains, iterative recursion or IPv6 transport is supplied. AAAA records
can still be queried over IPv4 DNS. A valid NXDOMAIN response is displayed
with a successful command status; failed queries exit nonzero.
.SH EXAMPLES
dig example.com MX
.br
dig @1.1.1.1 example.com A +short
.br
dig -x 192.168.64.1
.SH SEE ALSO
host(1), htget(1)
